name 'pl-aif-y2-u1-l3-return-values'
title 'title'
description 'description here'

markdown <<MARKDOWN
<h2>Return Values</h2>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;">
    <strong>&#128214; Return value:</strong> the output of a function.
  </div>
</div>

<p>A parameter is how a value goes into a function. A return value is how a value comes back out. Here are two different functions that return something.</p>

<div style="margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; color: #1F1976; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.04em; margin-bottom: 8px;">Example 1: Next Year's Age</div>
  <div style="border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8;">
    <div style="background-color: #1F1976; padding: 16px 18px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #E4E2F8;">
      <div style="line-height: 2;">def next_number(num):</div>
      <div style="line-height: 2;">&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; border-bottom: 3px solid #34BD43; padding: 1px 4px; border-radius: 3px;">return num + 1</span></div>
      <div style="height: 16px;"></div>
      <div style="line-height: 2;">age = 15</div>
      <div style="line-height: 2;">next_age = next_number(age)</div>
      <div style="line-height: 2;">print(f"Next year, I will be {next_age} years old.")</div>
    </div>
    <div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; padding: 12px 18px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #1F1976;">
      <div style="line-height: 1.9;">Next year, I will be 16 years old.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div style="margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; color: #1F1976; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.04em; margin-bottom: 8px;">Example 2: Steps to Miles</div>
  <div style="border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8;">
    <div style="background-color: #1F1976; padding: 16px 18px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #E4E2F8;">
      <div style="line-height: 2;">def steps_to_miles(steps):</div>
      <div style="line-height: 2;">&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; border-bottom: 3px solid #34BD43; padding: 1px 4px; border-radius: 3px;">return steps / 2000</span></div>
      <div style="height: 16px;"></div>
      <div style="line-height: 2;">miles = steps_to_miles(6000)</div>
      <div style="line-height: 2;">print(f"You walked {miles} miles.")</div>
    </div>
    <div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; padding: 12px 18px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #1F1976;">
      <div style="line-height: 1.9;">You walked 3.0 miles.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div style="line-height: 2;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;">Both functions do the same thing: they work something out and hand the answer back. The line that calls the function catches that answer in a variable, <code style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1; padding: 0 4px;">next_age</code> in one and <code style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1; padding: 0 4px;">miles</code> in the other. After that, the program can use it however it likes.</div>
</div>

<h2>Return vs. Print</h2>

<p>These two look alike when students first run them, but they do different jobs. Compare the same function written both ways.</p>

<div style="margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; color: #1F1976; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.04em; margin-bottom: 8px;">With print</div>
  <div style="border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8;">
    <div style="background-color: #1F1976; padding: 16px 18px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #E4E2F8;">
      <div style="line-height: 2;">def next_number(num):</div>
      <div style="line-height: 2;">&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; border-bottom: 3px solid #34BD43; padding: 1px 4px; border-radius: 3px;">print(num + 1)</span></div>
      <div style="height: 16px;"></div>
      <div style="line-height: 2;">next_age = next_number(15)</div>
      <div style="line-height: 2;">print(next_age)</div>
    </div>
    <div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; padding: 12px 18px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #1F1976;">
      <div style="line-height: 1.9;">16</div>
      <div style="line-height: 1.9;">None</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div style="margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; color: #1F1976; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.04em; margin-bottom: 8px;">With return</div>
  <div style="border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8;">
    <div style="background-color: #1F1976; padding: 16px 18px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #E4E2F8;">
      <div style="line-height: 2;">def next_number(num):</div>
      <div style="line-height: 2;">&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; border-bottom: 3px solid #34BD43; padding: 1px 4px; border-radius: 3px;">return num + 1</span></div>
      <div style="height: 16px;"></div>
      <div style="line-height: 2;">next_age = next_number(15)</div>
      <div style="line-height: 2;">print(next_age)</div>
    </div>
    <div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; padding: 12px 18px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #1F1976;">
      <div style="line-height: 1.9;">16</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.6;">
    <strong>The difference:</strong>
  </div>
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.6; margin-top: 8px;">&bull; <strong>print</strong> shows a value on the screen, then it's gone. The function gives nothing back, so <code style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1; padding: 0 4px;">next_age</code> ends up as <code style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1; padding: 0 4px;">None</code>, Python's word for "no value."</div>
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.6; margin-top: 6px;">&bull; <strong>return</strong> hands the value back to the code that called the function, so it can be stored and used again.</div>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;">
    <strong>&#128161; Teacher tip:</strong> if a student's function seems to work when they call it on its own but the result is <code style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1; padding: 0 4px;">None</code> when they store it, check for <code style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1; padding: 0 4px;">print</code> where <code style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1; padding: 0 4px;">return</code> belongs. Ask: "Do you want to show this value, or use it later?"
  </div>
</div>
MARKDOWN
